🚀 Week 5 – Day 6 Customized Exceptions & Chained Exceptions ☕

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🧠 Introduction

Java allows developers to create their own exceptions when built-in exceptions are not sufficient.

👉 This helps in:

✔ Better error handling
✔ Business rule validation
✔ Meaningful error messages

Java also supports **Exception Chaining**, which helps track the original cause of an exception.

---

🎯 Why Create Custom Exceptions?

Built-in exceptions may not always describe application-specific errors.

Examples:

* Invalid Age
* Insufficient Balance
* Invalid Employee ID
* Invalid Exam Marks

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🔹 1. Customized Exceptions

A customized (user-defined) exception is created by extending the `Exception` class.

---

📌 Syntax

```java id="c1"
class MyException extends Exception {

    MyException(String message) {
        super(message);
    }
}
```

---

# 💻 Example: Invalid Age Exception

```java id="c2"
class InvalidAgeException extends Exception {

    InvalidAgeException(String message) {
        super(message);
    }
}

public class Demo {

    static void validateAge(int age)
            throws InvalidAgeException {

        if (age < 18) {
            throw new InvalidAgeException(
                    "Age must be 18 or above");
        }

        System.out.println("Eligible");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        try {
            validateAge(15);
        }
        catch (InvalidAgeException e) {
            System.out.println(e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

---

📌 Output

```text id="c3"
Age must be 18 or above
```

---

🔹 Steps to Create Custom Exception

1️⃣ Create a class extending `Exception`
2️⃣ Create constructor
3️⃣ Use `throw`
4️⃣ Handle using `try-catch`

---

🏦 Real-World Example

```java id="c4"
class InsufficientBalanceException
        extends Exception {

    InsufficientBalanceException(String msg) {
        super(msg);
    }
}
```

