🔗 2. Chained Exceptions

Exception Chaining means associating one exception with another.

👉 Helps identify the root cause of an error.

---

📌 Syntax

```java id="ch1"
Throwable cause = new Exception();

Exception e =
        new Exception("New Exception", cause);
```

---

💻 Example

```java id="ch2"
public class Demo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        try {

            NumberFormatException cause =
                    new NumberFormatException(
                            "Invalid Number");

            throw new Exception(
                    "Application Error",
                    cause);

        }
        catch (Exception e) {

            System.out.println(
                    "Main Exception: " +
                    e.getMessage());

            System.out.println(
                    "Cause: " +
                    e.getCause());
        }
    }
}
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

📌 Output

```text id="ch3"
Main Exception: Application Error
Cause: java.lang.NumberFormatException:
Invalid Number
```

---

🔍 Why Use Chained Exceptions?

✔ Preserve original exception details
✔ Easier debugging
✔ Better error tracking

---

📌 Using initCause()

You can set the cause later.

```java id="ch4"
Exception e = new Exception("Main Error");

e.initCause(
        new ArithmeticException("Divide by Zero"));
```

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

📊 Custom Exception vs Built-in Exception

| Feature     | Custom Exception     | Built-in Exception |
| ----------- | -------------------- | ------------------ |
| Created By  | Developer            | Java               |
| Purpose     | Application-specific | General errors     |
| Flexibility | High                 | Limited            |

---

⚠️ Common Mistakes

❌ Extending wrong class
❌ Not calling `super(message)`
❌ Ignoring root cause in chained exceptions
❌ Using generic exception everywhere

---

🧠 Real Insight

👉 Most enterprise applications create custom exceptions for:

* Banking systems
* E-commerce platforms
* Hospital management systems
* Authentication systems

👉 Chained exceptions are widely used in frameworks such as:

* Spring
* Hibernate
* JDBC

